LAYOUT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The interface consists of a menu bar (located at the top), a graphical toolbar (located below the menu bar), the equation entry bar(located below the toolbar), the cell table (located in the middle), and the status bar (located at the very bottom). We will refer to the menu bar as 'menu', the graphical toolbar as 'toolbar', the equation entry bar as 'equation bar', the cell table as 'Cell area', and the status bar simply as 'status bar.' 

The cell area is organized in a grid system. The columns are marked with letters A-K and rows are marked with numbers 1-40. In total there are 440 cells to work with.

You can refer to cells by their coordinates within the program. To access the very first cell, for example, you would type the first column letter 'A' followed by the first row number '1.' Together, 'A1.'


OPENING AND SAVING FILES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

To open a new file, you can either click on the 'File' option on the menu and then click 'Open', click on the folder icon on the toolbar, or press Ctrl + O. A file manager should open up which will allow you to choose your desired file. You can only select a Discount Microsoft Excel™ file (.dme, more on that in section 7) at the moment.

To save a document, you can either click on the 'File' option on the menu and then click 'Save', click on the floppy disk icon on the toolbar, or press Ctrl + S. If saving a document for the first time, a file manager will appear asking you to name your document.

To make a new document, you can either click on the 'File' option on the menu and then click 'New', click on the paper with the plus sign icon on the toolbar, or press Ctrl + N. The program will ask you to save first, then all cells and cell formatting will be cleared.


EDITING CELLS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

NOTE: Multi-cell copying, cutting, and pasting is very basic. It works well within one column, but selecting multiple rows within multiple columns and then pasting them somewhere else WILL have unintended results. 

Copy - Select text within a cell, or while holding down the left mouse button, hover over multiple cells, then on the menu click 'Edit', and then 'Copy', or press Ctrl + C.

Cut - Select text within a cell, or while holding down the left mouse button, hover over multiple cells, then on the menu click 'Edit', and then 'Cut', or press Ctrl + X.

Paste - Select a cell then on the menu click 'Edit', and then 'Paste', or press Ctrl + V. If more than one cell was copied to the clipboard, the data will automatically be placed in the cells below it.


FORMATTING CELLS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Bold text - Highlight text within a cell, click 'Format' on the menu, and then click 'Bold.' You can also click on the large 'B' on the toolbar or press Ctrl + B.

Italicize text - Highlight text within a cell, click 'Format' on the menu and then click 'Italicize.' You can also click on the slanted 'I' on the toolbar or press Ctrl + I.

Underline text - Highlight text within a cell, then click 'Format' on the menu and then click 'Underline.' You can also click on the underlined 'U' on the toolbar or press Ctrl + U.

Strikethrough text - Highlight text within a cell then click 'Format' on the menu and then click 'Strikethrough.' You can also click on the strike-throughed 'T' on the toolbar or press Ctrl + T.

Text Color - Change color of text within a cell. Select a cell then from the menu, click 'Format' then click 'Text Color.' You can also click the 'Text Color' button on the toolbar.

Background Color - Change the background color of a cell. Select a cell then from the menu, click 'Format' then click 'Background Color.' You can also click the 'Background Color' button on the toolbar.


EQUATIONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Diagram 1: Overview of the equation bar
Note: Not to scale

Insert Button    Current Cell     Function Wizard   Equation Entry
   |                |                   |               |
   V                V                   V               V
|Insert|          |A1|          =    |⨍(x)|   |                   |


To select a cell in which to insert an equation, click on the desired cell or enter its coordinates in the 'Current Cell' entry box. Then you can write an equation in the 'Equation Entry' box. Equations are formatted as such: EQUATION(ARGS).  

Example:

|Insert|  |A4|  = |⨍(x)| |ADD(A1,A2,A3)|

Outputs:

    |      A      |

| 1 | 4           |
| 2 | 5           |
| 3 | 6           |
| 4 | 15          |


To select a range of cells, one can use the '::' operator. Example:

|Insert|  |A4|  = |⨍(x)| |ADD(A1::A3)|

Values can also be directly added to equations. Examples:

|Insert|  |A4| = |⨍(x)| |ADD(A1,A2,34)|

|Insert|  |A4| = |⨍(x)| |TIMEDIFF(A1,5:37)|

Pressing the |⨍(x)| button will bring up the function wizard. In the new window, you can select an equation from the list by double-clicking, modify it's arguments, then insert that equation into the equation entry. The equation Wizard will automatically try to solve your inputed equation as you type. It will print 'ERROR' if there is a problem with your equation.


Available equations:
ADD - Adds selected cells
SUB - Subtracts selected cells
MULTI - Multiplies selected cells
DIV - Divides selected cells
AVRG - Finds the average of the selected cells
TIMEDIFF - Finds the difference between two times
TIMEADD - Adds two time values together
PAYROLL - Multiplies a pay rate with a time (must be 24-hour format)
*Note: for operations involving time, the values MUST be formatted as a time. Values in brackets are optional:

12-hour: HH:MM[:SS] PM/AM
24-hour: HH:MM[:SS]

Examples:

    12:00 PM

    16:36

    14:21:34

    2:03:41 AM

All time equation outputs are in 24-hour format.


THE STATUS BAR
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

At the very bottom of the window you will see a status bar. The only thing on it is the last save time. Sorry, but that's all we could do.


WHAT IS .DME?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

After all the fun we had making up the .dmw file extension, we went ahead and made up another one: .dme. It stands for Discount Microsoft Excel™ File. This file format is currently the only way to save work within the program and may not work in any other program. In other words, don't use this program for anything important please (unless you plan to keep it forever or are a computer nerd). We are not responsible for lost data and we will laugh at you if you email to complain.
